Queue twice the usual decoded video frames on OHOS.

VideoRendererImpl decodes ahead until it holds initial_buffering_size_
frames, four by default. A phone here decodes YouTube's 10-bit VP9 in
software -- no VP9 hardware decoder, and libvpx threads nothing when a
stream has more than one tile row -- and on a Mate 70 Pro+ 1080p60 HDR took
9-10 ms a frame on average with runs of frames at 20-80 ms
(ohos-vp9-row-threads.patch logs them). The average has room to spare in a
60 fps frame's 16.7 ms; the runs emptied the four-frame queue, and a fifth
of the frames were dropped.

On OHOS the default is eight frames, inside the tuneable's own 3-10 range,
which gives a run 133 ms to be caught up in. It costs four more decoded
frames held (about 24 MB at 1080p 10-bit) and four more frames' decoding
before playback starts or resumes after a seek.

Generated against Chromium 154.0.8037.51.

Apply with:
  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-video-queue-depth.patch

diff --git a/media/renderers/video_renderer_impl.h b/media/renderers/video_renderer_impl.h
index 8ad3311..9b8bbe3 100644
--- a/media/renderers/video_renderer_impl.h
+++ b/media/renderers/video_renderer_impl.h
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 #include "base/task/sequenced_task_runner.h"
 #include "base/time/time.h"
 #include "base/timer/timer.h"
+#include "build/build_config.h"
 #include "media/base/decryptor.h"
 #include "media/base/demuxer_stream.h"
 #include "media/base/frame_rate_estimator.h"
@@ -347,8 +348,20 @@ class MEDIA_EXPORT VideoRendererImpl
   base::CancelableOnceClosure paint_first_frame_cb_;
 
   // The initial value for |min_buffered_frames_| and |max_buffered_frames_|.
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+  // Twice the usual, within the tuneable's range. A phone here decodes
+  // YouTube's 10-bit VP9 in software, on one thread when the stream has
+  // more than one tile row: 9-10 ms a frame on average at 1080p60 on a Mate
+  // 70 Pro+, but runs of frames at 20-80 ms, which emptied a 4-frame queue
+  // and dropped a fifth of the frames. Eight frames give those runs 133 ms
+  // to be caught up in.
+  Tuneable<size_t> initial_buffering_size_ = {
+      "MediaInitialBufferingSizeForHaveEnough", 3,
+      2 * limits::kMaxVideoFrames, 10};
+#else
   Tuneable<size_t> initial_buffering_size_ = {
       "MediaInitialBufferingSizeForHaveEnough", 3, limits::kMaxVideoFrames, 10};
+#endif
 
   // The number of frames required to transition from BUFFERING_HAVE_NOTHING to
   // BUFFERING_HAVE_ENOUGH.
